<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE CODING STANDARD.

    A GlobalPackageReference, so it applies to every project in the solution without any
    project restating it - which is the only form that makes "the same bar everywhere" a
    property of the build rather than of whoever remembered to add the reference. It is
    implicitly PrivateAssets="all" (a development dependency), so it reaches no published
    assembly and no transitive consumer.

    It replaces what used to be a hand-maintained .editorconfig plus a block of
    coding-standard properties in Directory.Build.props: analyzer severities, C# style,
    naming, the banned-API sets, and the MSBuild switches that make them fail a build
    (TreatWarningsAsErrors, EnforceCodeStyleInBuild, AnalysisLevel=latest-all,
    Nullable=enable, NuGetAudit at level `low` treated as errors). Five analyzer packages
    ride along: Meziantou.Analyzer, BannedApiAnalyzers, NetAnalyzers, SonarAnalyzer.CSharp
    and StyleCop.Analyzers.

    It is deliberately NOT in the AR-1 table below and NOT asserted by
    PackageVersionPinTests. AR-1 pins the stack the product is built FROM; this is build-time
    tooling that never ships, so bumping it is a developer decision rather than an
    architecture edit. The gate that guards it is the build itself: a version bump that
    introduces a rule the codebase violates fails immediately and loudly.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Coding standard: applies to every project, ships in none of them">
    <GlobalPackageReference Include="Opinionated.DotNet.CodingStandards" Version="0.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    AR-1's pinned stack. This file is the single place "every dependency is pinned to the
    AR-1 versions" (AC1) is expressed, and the single place the version-pin gate reads.

    Changing a version here is an architecture edit, not a developer decision: the AR-1
    table in epics.md changes first, then this file, then the expected-version table in
    tests/Yello.Tests.Architecture/PackageVersionPinTests.cs.

    Under central package management, projects declare <PackageReference Include="..." />
    with no Version attribute. Several packages below are pinned but not yet referenced by
    any project; the owning story adds the reference against the version pinned here.
  -->

  <!--
    DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND MUST STAY ABSENT (AC4):
    Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory.

    An in-memory provider cannot exercise row-level security, which is what NFR-1 rests
    on. Leaving it un-pinned centrally is the cheapest enforcement available: a project
    that references it fails to restore rather than failing a review. Gate A asserts both
    halves of the ban - no central version, and no reference from any project under tests/.
  -->

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: EF Core 10 - pinned here, first referenced by story 1.3">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: ASP.NET Core Identity 10 - pinned here, first referenced by story 1.3">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: Asp.Versioning.Http 10.0.0 - pinned here, first referenced by story 8.2">
    <PackageVersion Include="Asp.Versioning.Http" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    LOCALISATION. Not an AR-1 stack choice, and added by story 1.3.

    No acceptance criterion in that story mentions localisation. The build gate makes it
    unavoidable anyway: No_user_visible_string_literal_appears_in_a_component fails on any word
    of two or more letters that is not `Yello` in a .razor text node or in one of ten
    attributes, so a single `Email` label is a build failure. The gate's own remedy message
    names `IStringLocalizer` and `@Localizer["Key"]` as the idiom, and there was no
    localisation infrastructure in the repository at all.

    It needs a central pin because neither consumer inherits it. Yello.Client is a Blazor
    WebAssembly app, which resolves against Microsoft.NETCore.App and the WebAssembly packages
    rather than the ASP.NET Core shared framework - verified against the restored asset graph,
    which contains no Microsoft.Extensions.Localization at all. Yello.Infrastructure is a plain
    library and inherits no shared framework either. Yello.Host would get it free from
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web, and does not reference it.

    Version tracks the runtime patch pinned in Directory.Build.props, like the Blazor WASM row
    above and for the same reason: these ship as one band, and a Microsoft.Extensions.* package
    a patch out of step with the runtime is the kind of divergence nothing here would notice.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Localisation - pinned by story 1.3, referenced by Yello.Client and Yello.Infrastructure">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Localization" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The SQL driver, referenced directly by Yello.Host to satisfy AC4's "a working connection
    from Host to container", and pulled in transitively by Aspire.Hosting.SqlServer. Not an
    AR-1 stack choice and not an ORM.

    7.0.2 is the lowest version that satisfies BOTH consumers of it in this solution, which
    is why it is not simply EF Core's floor:
      * Aspire.Hosting.SqlServer 13.4.6 requires >= 7.0.1
      * Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer 10.0.11 requires >= 6.1.6
    Pinning EF Core's floor produced NU1109 ("detected package downgrade ... from 7.0.1 to
    centrally defined 6.1.6") once transitive pinning was on. Note precisely what disagrees:
    neither AR-1 pin names Microsoft.Data.SqlClient at all - it is their transitive FLOORS
    that conflict, and 7.0.2 is where both are satisfied. 7.0.2 is the newest 7.x stable;
    7.1.0 is still preview.

    Deliberately NOT the Aspire client integration (Aspire.Microsoft.Data.SqlClient): that
    package registers a database health check by default, and AR-33 requires liveness and
    readiness probes to answer from process state with no database round trip. The plain
    driver keeps the single connection this story opens fully explicit, with nothing on a
    timer and nothing attached to a probe.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="AC4: SQL driver for the one-shot connectivity check">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="7.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: Aspire 13.4 - referenced by Yello.AppHost (story 1.1)">
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.AppHost" Version="13.4.6" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Aspire.Hosting.SqlServer" Version="13.4.6" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: xunit.v3 4.0.0 - runs on Microsoft.Testing.Platform only">
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
    <!--
      The fixture-library half of xunit.v3, for Yello.Tests.Shared. The full `xunit.v3`
      metapackage refuses to build in a project that is not OutputType=Exe, which is
      correct: Shared holds fixtures, never test cases.

      Declaring it here also pins it TRANSITIVELY. Testcontainers.XunitV3 4.6.0 - the AR-1
      pin - was built against xunit.v3.extensibility.core 2.0.2, two major versions behind
      AR-1's xunit.v3 4.0.0. Left alone, NuGet would unify at 4.0.0 silently; pinning it
      explicitly makes the version the whole solution actually compiles against a stated
      fact. SqlServerContainerFixture is what proves the pair holds at compile time.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" Version="4.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: Testcontainers.XunitV3 4.6.0 - referenced by Yello.Tests.Shared">
    <PackageVersion Include="Testcontainers.XunitV3" Version="4.6.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Testcontainers.MsSql" Version="4.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    TRANSITIVE SECURITY PIN, not a stack choice.

    Testcontainers 4.6.0 (the AR-1 pin) depends on SSH.NET 2024.2.0, which carries
    GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284 - a HIGH severity advisory: "ScpClient Recursive Download Allows
    Arbitrary File Write via Server-Controlled SCP Filenames". Because warnings are errors,
    NuGet's NU1903 fails the build, which is the gate working as intended.

    The advisory covers everything up to and including SSH.NET 2025.1.0 and is first
    patched in 2026.0.0, so bumping Testcontainers would NOT have cleared it - the AR-1
    pin is not the cause and refreshing it is not the remedy. Transitive pinning is,
    which is why CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled is on above.

    Yello never calls ScpClient. Testcontainers loads SSH.NET only on its remote/SSH
    port-forwarding path; the local Docker path used here never touches it. The pin is
    therefore defence in depth, and it leaves the AR-1 table untouched: changing an AR-1
    pin is an architecture edit, and this is not one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Transitive security pin: GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284">
    <PackageVersion Include="SSH.NET" Version="2026.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: TngTech.ArchUnitNET 0.13.3 - referenced by Yello.Tests.Architecture">
    <PackageVersion Include="TngTech.ArchUnitNET" Version="0.13.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnitV3" Version="0.13.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    AR-1's "ASP.NET Core / Blazor WASM 10" row, and gated as one.

    These were previously labelled framework packages rather than stack choices and left out
    of the version-pin gate's expected table entirely. That was a misreading of the source:
    epics.md lists the row under "Pinned versions" and ARCHITECTURE-SPINE.md gives it its own
    line in the Stack table, so it is an AR-1 pin like any other. AC1's own enumeration drops
    the row while AR-1 keeps it, and the implementation had followed the paraphrase.

    The chosen value was right for the wrong reason: these ship with the Blazor WebAssembly
    SDK and must track the runtime patch pinned in Directory.Build.props, which is 10.0.11 -
    the same value AR-1 states. They are now in ExpectedPins and asserted like the rest.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="AR-1: ASP.NET Core / Blazor WASM 10 - referenced by Yello.Client">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.DevServer" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
